Digital was a leader in many cases until the mid 1980s, but starting in early
1980s, the "loser" portion started to grow within and eventually took over by
the end of the 1980s.

Ken Olsen's decision not to do a personal computer is tragic when you compare
it with the FULL picture.

Remember that IBM had refused to do a personal computer until Apple started to
grow with its APPLE II and started to make inroads into businesses because of
Visicalc. It saw the potential for its monopoly to erode and reluctantly
decided to embark into the personal computer market and win over businesses
with its IBM branded product.

If IBM understood the potential of personal computers. how come Digital didn't ?

Remember that Digital had, up until then, battled to make computing affordable
compared to IBM mainframes.


When Digital came out with decwidnows in the late 80s, it again had a chance
to re-enter the market with an impressive software library and decwindows
applications that already were better than what dos had. The big battle might
have been between dec and apple.

Instead, DEC chose to prevent its low end machines from being too cheap so
that its large customers woudln't buy those smaller machines instead of the
expensive big ones.

In fairness, DEC had clustering, the PCs didn't. IBM didn't fear customers
buying PCs to replace mainframes.

The IPOD is another example whose history is in the making. It innovated and
is taking over a market. But will Apple protect its market at the expense of
losing it entirely (like DEC did, or will it open up its architecture like IBM
did and at least let the architecture flourish even if it doesn't have 100% of
it ?


