I am Richard Tomkins.
I worked in the Digital Equipment Corporation manufacturing plant in Kanata,
Ontario Canada, from 1986 until 2001.
My jobs were, Manufacturing Support Technologist, Product Engineer, Process
Engineer, Trainer, Consultant and CSS Technology Architect.

The plant was very diverse when I joined, making backplanes for PDP and VAX
computer systems and building various VAX computer sytems, such as the
11/750, 11/780, 9250, 8350, 8550. We had a development laboratory that
designed all the backplanes used in all the VAX computers, including the ill
timed VAX 9000 backplane modules. Over time we added the manufacture of
boards for VAX computers and OEM models and also took on the maufacture of
boards for the network gear.

I worked in an elite technology group that oversaw, designed and managed the
technology used in the manufacturing operations, there were 8 of us. We
designed, installed and managed the network that drove our operations in
boards and systems. We designed and managed the computing complex (VAX
cluster) that performed automated testing of all our VAX and PC systems, and
boards.

The VAX Fault Tolerant Computers were maufactured exclusively in our plant.

The early forays into the PC world happened under customer single source
demands. Lots of things were tried, the VT100 thingy, the DECmate, the
Rainbow and others. Oue first real foray into PC's on a serious level was
with Tandy as a partner. We also did a partner ship with Ollivetti, low end
desktops and laptops and notebooks. The Ollivetti laptops and notebooks were
all recalled.

As the corporation consolidated its manufacturing operations over the years,
more and more product was built in Kanata.

We did our own low cost PC's by going to a one of our disk plants in Tainwan
and go tthem to cook up a strong little groups of systems, the so-called
Tiger series.

I was the Product Engineer for the MicroVAX 3100 model 10 and 20, the
InfoServer 100, and 150.

We also built products for Apple Computer in Kanata, desktops, laser
printers and sub-assemblies.

The last VAX shipped from Kanata in 1999.

The Kanata plant was the sole source plant in North America for PC
production from about 1996 onward.

I was a member of the Technical Group ATF (Advanced Technology Forum) that
designed and developed FIS (Factory Installed Software) for ULTRIX, OpenVMS,
OSF/1, then Tru64 UNIX and of course, Windows and company, NextStep, Novell,
SCO Unix and others. This was an interplant group that met every two months
or so for a few days at different sites worlwide. I secured the budget for
the group one year in a presentation to the corporate folks. I was told I
had 15 minutes for my presentation and money was tight, I'd be lucky to see
$250k. I spent almost 2 hours in the presentation and was given $1.2
million.

We paid a Royalty to Microsoft on every computer sold if it could run a MS
OS. About 40% of the product was shipped without a Microsoft product, but we
still paid the Royalty. Take it or find another Windows source was
Microsofts argument. Of course, there was no negotiation on this. When the
Netscape guy said that dealing with MS was like the Godfather, one day you
expect to wake up with a horses head in your bed, we all nodded with
experience.

Windows NT development on Alpha was paid for entirely by Digital and
employed Digital staff.

Module production was an exclusive domain of the Albeququre New Mexico plant
at one time. Some bright weenie got the idea that oyu coudl make modules in
Mexco cheaper. It took almost two years to move the entire assembly line to
Mexico and the close the Alebququere plant. After struggling with
sub-standard quality for 6 months, we moved the entire operation from Mexco
to Kanata in 3 months and substantially rasied the quality levels to where
Albequequere had them before. Mexico was a stupid idea. Graft, corruption,
staff being threatened with harm, arbitrary jail terms for not paying off
government officials, poor workers, bad work ethic, no concept of ISO 9000
standards. Need I say more.

I probably could fill a book with what I know and what I have been privy to
hear. Like the time Larry Ellison wanted us out of the Video Server
business, so he'd stop making RDB for OpenVMS if we didn't clear out of
town..

Digital had an open door policy, or so they claimed. I have personanly
communicated with Bob Plamer on a number of issues while I worked there. I
may or may not have positively influenced products to some degree.

The HiNote Laptops were made by Citizen Watch Company. We moved in to their
production facility right after Comapq beat a retreat from them. Left most
of thier stuff behind and wrote it off, we brought the Citizen Watch Company
into the world of Quality Product Manufacturing.

Digital invented the Water Wash systems for Electronic Modules and as a good
corporate citizen of the world gave the technology to everyone for free.










