> Very old DEC "Flip Chip" cards as well as many more modern cards for
> PDP8s and PDP11s were coded with a color as well as a letter (part of the
> card part #) as to the type of logic or type of card.  Ones I know are:

> M Magenta        TTL IC logic and gate arrays (VERY numerous in PDP11s)
> R Red            RTL logic
> S Red (scarlet?) Faster RTL
> A Amber          Analog cards
> W White          Do-it-yourself or interface cards
> G Green          ?
> B Blue           ?
> K blacK          Some sort of controller interface.

G was analogue stuff that was part of a system, hence often found in
core memory board sets. Some of the memorys also had boards that just
had a H part number.

B, R, and S are variants of the same stuff. Will see if I can find the
details and post or get a pointer. There was the base family, stuff
used only in computers, and tweeked up faster cards. Can't remember
for sure which is what.

K was a slowed down set of logic for use in industrial control systems
and data aquisition. Yeah, DEC created that and lost it as well...

