You mean internally to the PSU or somewhere in the rest of the box ?

In some ways I'd like to be able to fix them, but having seen the pretty 
energetic arcing that characterises three out of the four failures, I 
find it hard to imagine that there isn't extensive damage to 
semiconductors with that much energy flying about.  Given the component 
count, I suspect that it might be horribly time consuming to identify 
and test all the components that might have failed, replacement 
components may be quite expensive, and there might remain significant 
residual risk of not understanding the root cause, and the problem 
immediately recurring.


As such, at least for the moment, I've succeeded in hacking a 650W ATX 
supply into the case of the old Alpha power supply, thereby conserving 
access to the main +12, +5 and +3.3 volt connections, and the machine 
starts up when you enable the ATX.  So far it gets as far as trying to 
boot NT, but can't as the wrong disks are in it at the moment.  But, it 
looks like a fix, and the new 650W ATX supply probably costs less than 
the new semiconductors would to repair the old power supply.

What I haven't done yet is linked in the front panel power switch and 
side panel interlock, which may be possible but isn't that vital.

More significant is that I'd like to connect the -12 volt rail just in 
case anything uses it, though the only thing that I can think of that 
might, is RS232 serial driver chips.  Anything else ?

Anyway -  many thanks to those who have helped !


Cheers, J/.
-- 
John Beardmore


