encased in wrap-around aluminum frames with a big blue Amphenol
connector on the back. Each had a 4 digit number and came in 5 MHz and
10 MHz versions

>Very old DEC "Flip Chip" cards as well as many more modern cards for
>PDP8s and PDP11s were coded with a color as well as a letter (part of the
>card part #) as to the type of logic or type of card.  Ones I know are:
>
>M Magenta        TTL IC logic and gate arrays (VERY numerous in PDP11s)
Yes, first use: the PDP-8/I for Integrated, the series continued
through the PDP-11s and later 8s, even after the flip-chip handle had
been replaced with a lock-down steel frame.
Used in the 8/I, later 10s, 11s, 12s, and 15s
>R Red            RTL logic
>S Red (scarlet?) Faster RTL
No: Special <to the original PDP-8 - with higher fan-out. You can
replace an R with an S and all is well, but the other way doesn't
alays work.
>A Amber          Analog cards
and A/D D/A paraphenalia
>W White          Do-it-yourself or interface cards
Also cables, bus cards, lamp drivers
>G Green          
Memory and power control
>B Blue           
A faster R - extensively used in the PDP-9 and KA-10
>K blacK          Some sort of controller interface.
Not quite, a shielded M card or special purpouse TTL->Industrial
driver used in roll-your-owns, PDP-14 and -16 industrial controllers.
I believe some were pin-to-pin compatible with Ms of the same number 
>
>Is there a complete list anywhere?

Unless someone has compiled one, no. DEC pspecial-purpose cards were
documented in the machines they were part of, the general-purpose run
in the annual Logic Handbooks.

The only color unmentioned was grey - a simple handle supplied to
those rolling their own meaning (nothing DEC but the handle)

The numbers on the R/B/M s did generally tell general function

where C is color and n is number

C-0nn - simple arrays of diodes
C-1nn - simple gates, NOTS and the like
C-2nn - flip-flops and slices of CPUs
C-3nn - delay lines and the like
C-4nn - clocks
C-5nn/6nn etc - special complex functions

>Do later cards such as VAX L or T cards have a "color"?  I know many
>M series cards had metal handles but they still stamped the part # on
>them and inked the stamped numbers magenta.

No, the color system was dead and they just needed codes for 'em
>I vaguely remember seeing a core memory card with a green handle and a H 
>part number (usually power supplies or cabinets).  Is there such a thing
>as a H color?  Did R and S cards use the same color?
I've never seen a G card with an H number, but it "should" be a power
supply component if it exists.
R&S were the same shade of red.
H stood for Hardware  & power supplies and had no color (or rather
several - the initial tweed brown and blue, followed by black, with
each machine given its own color scheme (12s were green/lime green,
11s were red/magenta, etc...) followed by white <g>

